```

The mutable default argument causes this. `tags=DEFAULT_TAGS` binds the parameter to the same list object on every call, so `tags.append("post")` mutates `DEFAULT_TAGS` itself. The first test run leaves it as `["draft", "post"]`; any earlier test that also called `make_post` without a `tags` argument appends another `"post"`, so by the time this test runs, the list has extra entries and the equality check fails.

Fix it by giving each call a fresh list:

```python
def make_post(title, tags=None):
    if tags is None:
        tags = list(DEFAULT_TAGS)
    tags.append("post")
    return {"title": title, "tags": tags}
```

This avoids sharing state across calls, which also protects any caller who passes in their own list.

